---
// The public moderation policy (decided 2026-09-29): there is no human moderator on duty, so what
// readers ask is decided by published rules, in the safe direction, and nothing waits forever. The
// rules it states are the code's: oscr/moderation.py (the Mac, which decides), src/lib/moderation.ts
// (the base rules, which the site tells the requester; both read tests/fixtures/moderation_rules.json),
// the Worker's own checks (worker/contributions/). science.css only.
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { CAMPAIGN, CAMPAIGN_DAYS, HIDE_PER_ACCOUNT, HIDE_PER_DAY, POLL_MINUTES, REVIEW_DAYS } from "../../lib/moderation";
import { NIGHTLY } from "../../lib/removal";

const ISSUES = "https://github.com/NeuraCrypt/OSCR/issues";
---
<Info
  title="How requests are decided"
  description={`How ${SITE_NAME} decides removal requests, submissions and claims: published rules, what waits for the operator and for how long, how to appeal. There is currently no human moderator on duty.`}
  group="policies"
  path="/policies/moderation/"
  parents={[{ text: "Policies", href: "/policies/" }]}
>
  <p class="summary">
    <strong>There is currently no human moderator on duty.</strong> The published rules below decide what readers ask of
    {" "}{SITE_NAME} (to remove something, to add a paper's code, to be recognized as a paper's author), and{" "}
    {SITE_NAME}'s own machine applies them. When in doubt, the rules choose the safer outcome: hiding is automatic, and
    publishing needs a verified identity or checks that pass. The operator, who runs {SITE_NAME}, reviews what the rules
    cannot decide when available, and can reverse what they did. No request waits more than {REVIEW_DAYS} days, except a
    request about personal data, which the operator must answer within one month (the GDPR) and does not close without
    an answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="when">When requests are decided</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>{SITE_NAME}'s machine reads the new requests about every {POLL_MINUTES} minutes and applies the rules at once. The
      decision, and its words, show on the request's page and on your account page. No email is sent.</li>
    <li>What a decision removes or adds reaches the site at its next nightly publication, at {NIGHTLY} (the registry's
      local time).</li>
    <li>Every automatic decision is recorded with the rule that made it, so that the operator can check it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="removals">Removal requests</h2>
  <p>
    A removal request names what to remove: a paper's whole record, the copies of its authors' scripts (or their display
    from their source: <a href="/policies/code/">the code policy</a>), one repository's, one file's, or its tracing map.
  </p>
  <dl class="review">
    <dt>From a verified author of the paper</dt>
    <dd>
      Applied at once, whatever it names. You are a verified author when you signed in with the ORCID iD
      the paper lists among its authors (or your claim of authorship was verified).
    </dd>
    <dt>From a maintainer of the code it names</dt>
    <dd>
      Applied at once for the copies of the repositories you maintain as their owner or a public member of their
      organization (checked on GitHub), or as the operator made you. A contributor is also recognized as a maintainer on
      GitHub, but a single merged pull request makes someone a contributor, so a contributor's request follows the rules
      for anyone.
    </dd>
    <dt>Copies of the authors' code, for copyright or personal data, from anyone</dt>
    <dd>
      Hidden at once, until the operator reviews the request. The operator may restore what the request did not
      justify. To prevent abuse, at most {HIDE_PER_ACCOUNT} such requests from one account and {HIDE_PER_DAY} in all are
      hidden at once in a day; a justification sent with {CAMPAIGN} requests or more in {CAMPAIGN_DAYS} days, or a request the
      operator already refused, waits for the operator.
    </dd>
    <dt>Anything else</dt>
    <dd>
      A whole record, a tracing map, or copies for another reason, from someone the registry cannot verify: nothing is
      hidden, and the request waits for the operator, {REVIEW_DAYS} days at most. If no one has reviewed it after{" "}
      {REVIEW_DAYS} days, it is closed without removal, with how to ask again. A record is built from the paper's own
      public metadata and links. If anyone's word were enough to remove it, anyone could erase anyone's work, so removing
      it needs a verified identity or the operator's decision.
    </dd>
    <dt>The same, asked for personal data</dt>
    <dd>
      Nothing is hidden in this case either, and the request waits for the operator. It is a request under the GDPR, so
      the operator answers it within one month, and the rules do not close it without an answer. It stays at the top of the
      operator's list, with its deadline.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="data-rights">Requests about your own data</h2>
  <p>
    Access to your data, the erasure of your contact details, an objection to their keeping, their rectification and the
    deletion of your account are asked on <a href="/data-rights/">your data and your rights</a>, signed in, and decided the
    same way, within about {POLL_MINUTES} minutes. The registry answers by itself what it can prove: access, erasure and
    objection for an account signed in with its ORCID iD (the iD is what ties an author's contact details to them), and
    the deletion of an account. The other requests (a rectification, or contact details asked for by an account without
    an ORCID iD, which a name, a GitHub login or a Google account cannot prove) wait for the operator, who answers within
    one month and does not close them without an answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="submissions">Submissions of a paper and its code</h2>
  <dl class="review">
    <dt>From an author of the paper</dt>
    <dd>Published once you publish its draft (your ORCID iD among the paper's authors).</dd>
    <dt>From anyone else</dt>
    <dd>
      Published when something the submitter cannot write proves that each code link belongs to the paper: the paper
      itself cites it (its text, or the metadata its publisher deposited at Crossref; or an archive the paper cites names
      it as its source), or its owner is proven to be one of the paper's authors (an author's public ORCID record links
      to the owner's GitHub account, or a verified author of the paper owns the repository: its owner, or a public member
      of its organization, on GitHub). A README that cites the paper, or a GitHub display name that bears an author's
      name, does not count as proof, because anyone can write them. Otherwise the submission waits for the operator,{" "}
      {REVIEW_DAYS} days at most, and the submitter is told why; if no one reviews it, it is closed with how to ask
      again. A paper
      outside neuroscience is refused.
    </dd>
    <dt>A draft not published</dt>
    <dd>Closed after {REVIEW_DAYS} days; it can be corrected and published again from your account page.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="claims">Claims</h2>
  <dl class="review">
    <dt>That you are a paper's author</dt>
    <dd>
      Verified at once when the paper lists your ORCID iD among its authors, or when Crossref's automatic update put the
      paper in your ORCID record (its publisher deposited your iD with it), checked again every day. A work you added to
      your ORCID record yourself, or through a search tool, does not count, because anyone can add any paper. Otherwise,
      the claim is closed after {REVIEW_DAYS} days with how to claim again.
    </dd>
    <dt>That you maintain a repository</dt>
    <dd>
      Verified at once when GitHub shows you as its owner, a public member of its organization or a contributor. Other
      forges are not checked automatically: such a claim waits for the operator, {REVIEW_DAYS} days at most, then is closed
      with how to be checked again. Only an owner or an organization member (or a maintainer the operator made) can correct
      a paper's record as its maintainer or have a removal applied at once. A contributor cannot.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="verified-only">What only verified people can do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A verified author of the paper can correct a record's links, and so can a maintainer of its code (its owner, or a
      public member of its organization) for their own repository. Every correction becomes a new version of the record,
      which says a verified person made it.</li>
    <li>A verified author, with their ORCID iD, can validate a tracing map and have it deposited on Zenodo.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="text">Free text</h2>
  <p>
    No text a reader types appears on a public page: the notes, statements, justifications and evidence links of
    requests are read by the operator only, and lose any email address. The only name of an account that can become
    public (the creator of a tracing map, when the paper does not list the validator's ORCID iD) is kept only when it
    looks like a name (no address, no link). For this reason the site uses no automatic text filter.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every request needs an account; per account and per day: 10 removal requests, 10 submissions, 20 corrections, 10
      validations, 10 claims (and 20 claims waiting at once).</li>
    <li>One removal request per account and record: complete it while it is open; once refused, it can be asked again only in
      a way the rules decide at once (as a verified author, as a maintainer, or for the copies only).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="appeal">How to appeal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      If the rules refused or closed a request, ask again in a way the rules decide: sign in with the ORCID iD the paper
      lists, verify your repository on GitHub from your account page, link your GitHub account from your ORCID record, or
      ask for the copies only. The operator can also override the rules and accept it.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the rules did something wrong (copies hidden without reason, a submission or a claim accepted by mistake), the
      operator reviews the automatic decisions and can reverse any of them.
    </li>
    <li>
      For anything else, use the project's <a href={ISSUES}>public issue tracker</a>. It is public, so put no personal
      data there. For your own data, use <a href="/data-rights/">your data and your rights</a>, and for personal data in
      a record, use the removal request page of the record, whose justification only the operator reads.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Info>
